{
  "dom_id": "step-q149651",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ |  |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ |  |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,1) & ={f}_{X}(4)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
